[center][size=6][b]ZERO COMPANY MOD COMMAND[/b][/size]
[size=3][color=#4fd1ff]The dedicated mod manager & launcher for STAR WARS: Zero Company[/color][/size]
[size=2]Steam & EA App · Windows & Linux/Steam Deck · Install · Organize · Detect conflicts · Launch[/size][/center]

[line]

[size=5][b]WHAT IS IT?[/b][/size]

Zero Company Mod Command is a standalone mod manager and launcher built specifically for [b]STAR WARS: Zero Company[/b], wrapped in a Star Wars holo-terminal interface. It installs every mod format this game has, runs guided installers, keeps your load order under control, proves [i]exactly[/i] which mods conflict with each other, protects your game files with backups and checksums, and launches the game — all from one window.

It finds your game automatically whether you bought it on [b]Steam or the EA App[/b], and it runs on [b]Windows or Linux/Steam Deck[/b]. No installation, no dependencies: download, run, mod.

[center][size=4][color=#6dffb0][b]★ ONE COMMUNITY, EVERY PLATFORM[/b][/color][/size]
[size=2]Steam and EA App players install the same mods from the same page — the manager detects your edition,
deploys identically on both, and warns EA players when a mod is Steam-only (and vice versa).
Windows gets the portable exe; Linux and Steam Deck get the AppImage.[/size][/center]

[size=5][b]KEY FEATURES[/b][/size]

[b][color=#ffc857]Mod management[/color][/b]
[list]
[*][b]One-window installs[/b] — drag & drop .zip / .7z / .rar archives, loose .pak / .utoc / .ucas files, or extracted folders anywhere onto the app
[*][b]Every format auto-detected[/b] — pak, IoStore, LogicMods, UE4SS Lua/DLL mods, game-folder replacement mods (e.g. movies), even the UE4SS runtime itself
[*][b]Guided installers (FOMOD)[/b] — mods that ship an installer script are installed by answering the author's own questions, with descriptions, images, recommended answers and conditional steps. Scripts are read, never executed
[*][b]Multi-mod archives[/b] — an archive holding several mods installs each as its own entry, so you can enable, order, update and remove them separately; updates replace every entry from the same download
[*][b]Your mods survive everything[/b] — the mod archive lives in the game folder (movable in Settings): delete or update the app and a fresh install restores every mod, profile and archived version automatically
[*][b]Bring your existing setup aboard[/b] — a one-time scan runs by itself after the first game connection, and Import existing covers the rest: mods installed by hand, orphaned archive entries, previous Mod Command data, and other managers' libraries (each mod imported separately, Nexus identities reattached by md5); anything matching a Nexus upload gets its updates tracked
[*][b]Game files protected[/b] — a replacement-style mod backs up the original of every game file it overwrites and restores it on disable/uninstall
[*][b]Clean enable/disable[/b] — canonical copies are kept in the manager's library, so toggling a mod never loses files. Every deployed file's SHA-256 is recorded; a file you edited by hand is never silently deleted
[*][b]Squad profiles[/b] — save named sets of enabled mods + load order and swap between them in two clicks; profiles remember each mod's [i]version[/i] and swap it back in from the vault when you apply
[*][b]Version vault[/b] — every update archives the old version; one click rolls a mod back (or forward again) to any archived version — and the Holonet's ⧗ picker can install [i]any[/i] version a mod's Nexus page offers
[*][b]Enable / Disable all[/b] — one button for the whole hangar, with the same file-safety checks as single toggles
[/list]

[b][color=#ffc857]Conflict detection that actually proves it[/color][/b]
[list]
[*][b]CONFIRMED conflicts[/b] — the manager reads the asset list inside each IoStore mod and flags mods that modify the [i]same game assets[/i], showing you the exact overlapping files
[*][b]SUSPECTED conflicts[/b] — matching file names across mods that can't be deep-scanned
[*][b]UE4SS hook scan[/b] — Lua mods are scanned for RegisterHook / RegisterKeyBind targets; two mods hooking the same function or binding the same key get reported, including mods you installed by hand
[*][b]Compatibility matrix[/b] — one grid showing every enabled mod against every other: ✔ compatible, ▲ suspected, ✖ confirmed
[/list]

[b][color=#ffc857]Load order[/color][/b]
[list]
[*]Drag-to-reorder pak/IoStore mods — later loads win conflicts
[*][b]Suggest order[/b] — one click proposes an order (broad overhauls first, targeted patches later) and tells you which conflicts it decides
[*][b]Review before apply[/b] — applying first shows every conflict pair and which winners change; [b]Undo last apply[/b] rolls the order back in one click
[*][b]UE4SS start order[/b] — drag your Lua/DLL mods; the manager writes one managed block into mods.txt (kept just before Keybinds, warning attached), preserves everything else, and tags each mod's DLL/Lua start pass
[*][b]Startup recovery[/b] — enabled mods whose deployed files went missing (game update, manual cleanup) are redeployed automatically
[/list]

[b][color=#ffc857]Steam AND EA App — one mod community[/color][/b]
[list]
[*][b]EA App edition detected and supported[/b] — the manager finds the EA install, deploys every mod format identically, and launches the EA edition directly
[*][b]EA compatibility flags[/b] — mods can declare Steam-only in their modinfo.json, and a live community list flags known cases: EA players get a clear warning before enabling, Steam players see which of their mods EA friends can't run
[*][b]Game-build warnings[/b] — every mod remembers which game build it was installed under; after a game update, possibly-broken mods are flagged until you mark them verified
[*][b]Game update freeze[/b] [i](experimental, opt-in)[/i] — stop the game auto-updating overnight and breaking your modded playthrough: one toggle sets Steam's update-on-launch-only mode, locks the manifest, and launches the game directly. Fully reversible, with the limits stated up front
[*][b]Linux / Steam Deck[/b] — Steam library discovery on Linux (native and flatpak), Proton prefix detection, UE4SS DLL-override guidance, and a ready-to-run AppImage in the Files tab
[/list]

[b][color=#ffc857]Updates, handled[/color][/b]
[list]
[*][b]Mod update checks[/b] — every Nexus and GitHub install is tracked; outdated mods get flagged in the Hangar Bay (checked automatically, or on demand)
[*][b]One-click updates[/b] — GitHub mods (and Nexus premium) update in place, keeping your load order, custom names, and enabled state; free Nexus accounts get a direct link and the returning download replaces the old version automatically
[*][b]Launcher updates[/b] — the app announces new launcher versions with a banner, so you always know when a better build is out
[/list]

[b][color=#ffc857]Two mod sources built in[/color][/b]
[list]
[*][b]Holonet browser[/b] — browse this game's entire Nexus catalogue inside the app: thumbnails, search, category filters, and sorting by downloads / endorsements / newest / updated. Mods you already have show a green IN HANGAR badge — and an Update button right on the card when a new version is out
[*][b]The Forge[/b] — a second tab of curated GitHub mods, hand-vetted by Envian Mods (the list updates live). Installs come only from release archives, behind a confirmation naming the exact repo and file
[*][b]nxm:// one-click downloads[/b] — press "Mod Manager Download" on the website and the mod downloads and installs straight into the manager
[*]Works with free accounts (via the website button) and premium accounts (fully in-app)
[/list]

[b][color=#ffc857]Quality of life[/color][/b]
[list]
[*][b]Datapad[/b] — built-in config editor for Engine.ini, GameUserSettings.ini, Input.ini, UE4SS-settings.ini and mod config files, with a structured key/value view that preserves comments and formatting, plus automatic first-save backups
[*][b]UE4SS one-click install[/b] — downloads and installs the latest experimental UE4SS runtime for Lua/DLL mod support
[*][b]Diagnostics[/b] — full health scan: game detection, launcher & build, deployed-file audit, tool availability, conflict report
[*][b]Support reports[/b] — one click copies a complete, [i]sanitized[/i] bug report (mod list, conflicts, health scan, session log) with your paths, username and machine name scrubbed — paste it straight into a Posts-tab report
[*]Your Nexus API key is stored encrypted with your OS account — never in plain text
[*]Launch via Steam (overlay + cloud saves), the EA edition directly, or a direct exe launch
[/list]

[size=5][b]GETTING STARTED[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Grab your build from the Files tab: [b]Windows[/b] → the portable zip (extract anywhere, run ZeroCompanyModCommand.exe) · [b]Linux / Steam Deck[/b] → the AppImage (make it executable and run it, Desktop Mode on Deck)
[*]The game is auto-detected — Steam and EA App installs both (or set the folder in Settings)
[*]Already modded? [b]Hangar Bay → Import existing[/b] adopts your current mods without moving a file
[*]In Settings → Nexus Mods, paste your personal API key and press [b]Register handler[/b] to enable one-click downloads
[*]Install mods from the Holonet tab, or drag archives onto the window
[/list]

[b]Requirements:[/b] Windows 10/11 (portable exe) or Linux/Steam Deck (AppImage) · STAR WARS: Zero Company (Steam or EA App) · optional: 7-Zip / p7zip for .7z/.rar archives

[size=5][b]FOR MOD AUTHORS[/b][/size]

Make your mod first-class in the manager with a tiny [b]modinfo.json[/b] next to your files:
[code]{ "title": "My Cool Mod", "version": "1.2", "author": "You",
  "description": "One line about it", "eaCompatible": true }[/code]
[list]
[*][b]title / version / author / description[/b] — shown in the Hangar Bay instead of the archive filename, and per entry in multi-mod archives
[*][b]eaCompatible[/b] (or [b]"launchers": ["steam"][/b]) — tells EA App players up front whether your mod works for them; works for Steam-only declarations too
[*][b]FOMOD installers[/b] are fully supported — ship ModuleConfig.xml/info.xml and users get your guided steps, images and conditional options
[*][b]Multi-mod archives[/b] — put each mod in its own folder and every one installs as a separate, individually-updatable entry
[*]Want your GitHub-hosted mod in [b]The Forge[/b]? Suggest it on the Discord below
[/list]

[size=5][b]FAQ[/b][/size]

[b]Q: I bought the game on the EA App — does this work for me?[/b]
A: Yes. The manager detects the EA edition automatically, every mod format deploys exactly as on Steam, and the Launch button starts the EA edition directly. Mods flagged as Steam-only show a clear warning before you enable them.

[b]Q: Does it run on Steam Deck?[/b]
A: Yes — download the AppImage, run it in Desktop Mode, and mods you deploy work in Gaming Mode. Diagnostics walks you through the one UE4SS launch-option line Proton needs (the manager never edits your launch options itself).

[b]Q: Does it work with mods I installed manually?[/b]
A: Yes — [b]Hangar Bay → Import existing[/b] adopts paks in ~mods, LogicMods and UE4SS mod folders in place, without moving or renaming anything. Adopted files that match a Nexus upload are identified automatically so updates get tracked; the rest can be linked by hand from their LOCAL badge.

[b]Q: Windows SmartScreen warns about the exe.[/b]
A: The exe is unsigned (code-signing certificates are expensive). Click "More info → Run anyway". The app is fully self-contained and only writes inside its own folder, your game folder, and the game's config folder.

[b]Q: Can it break my game?[/b]
A: Mods deploy only to the standard mod locations, game-folder mods back up every original file they replace and restore them on disable, and every deployed file's checksum is tracked — a file you changed by hand is never silently deleted. Disabling or uninstalling a mod removes exactly what it deployed, and config files are backed up automatically on first save.

